-**Subtopics:**
-*   **Ellipsoidal Footprints:** Uses the min-volume enclosing ellipsoid to reduce overdraw. Sets it apart by providing a tighter fit than circular footprints, improving image quality at the cost of more complex fragment culling [S1]. Choose when high fidelity is needed and overdraw is a concern [S1].
-*   **Circular Footprints:** Uses a simple circle in screen space. Sets it apart by being computationally cheaper than ellipsoids but with higher overdraw [S1]. Choose for faster interaction or simpler hardware [S1].
-*   **Direct Rendering (Sibling):** Renders tetrahedra as faces. Sets it apart by avoiding approximation error but generating excessive fragments for varying tetrahedron sizes [S1].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Sub-pixel tetrahedra:** Standard triangular/quadrilateral primitives are not optimal for sub-pixel sized areas due to connectivity overhead. Point-based rendering solves this by using single points [S1].
+*   **Boundary Quality:** Boundary quality degrades because the single-point representation lacks scalar interpolation across the tetrahedron, limiting accurate pixel values when depth complexity is low [S1].
+*   **Malformed Tetrahedra:** Malformed tetrahedra cause Hardware-Assisted Visibility Sorting (HAVS) to produce erroneous renderings, but this point-based method remains robust and does not fail under these conditions [S1].
+*   **Wide Size Variation:** If tetrahedra sizes vary widely, directly rendering the mesh may be better than approximating projections, as point sizes for large tetrahedra increase more rapidly than for small ones, increasing fragment generation. However, culling in the fragment program can mitigate this, though testing and branching is expensive [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Sorting is $O(N \log N)$ where $N$ is the number of tetrahedra [S1]. Rendering is dominated by fragment processing, which is $O(F)$ where $F$ is the number of fragments generated [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store point attributes [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Achieves interactive framerates for large datasets (e.g., 3.5M tetrahedra) [S1]. Performance scales with the number of fragments; ellipsoidal footprints reduce overdraw compared to circular or square footprints [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** No connectivity processing; robust to malformed tetrahedra; easy LOD implementation; avoids convolution [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Approximation error; boundary quality degrades due to lack of scalar interpolation across the tetrahedron; sub-pixel tetrahedra are handled well, but large variations in tetrahedron size can lead to excessive fragment generation if not managed by LOD [S1].
-*   **When to use:** Interactive visualization of large unstructured tetrahedral meshes [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** When exact boundary representation is critical and approximation error is unacceptable; when hardware does not support programmable shaders for compositing [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The preprocessing step is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of tetrahedra. Per-frame, sorting is $O(N \log N)$ or $O(N)$ with efficient spatial sorting. GPU processing is $O(N)$ for vertex shading and fragment generation, with compositing being $O(F)$ where $F$ is the number of fragments.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the point representations and transforms. No connectivity information is stored [S1].
+*   **Measured Performance:** The method achieves interactive framerates for large datasets (e.g., Heart, SPX, fighter, F16) [S1]. It is generally faster than current direct volume rendering approaches [S1].
+*   **Scaling:** Accuracy scales with approximation complexity; higher approximation (e.g., ellipsoidal vs. circular) yields better visual fidelity but increases fragment generation and decreases performance [S1].
+*   **Strengths:**
+    *   Eliminates the need to process/store connectivity information [S1].
+    *   Eases LOD implementation [S1].
+    *   Robust to malformed tetrahedra [S1].
+    *   Avoids convolution operations [S1].
+    *   Flexible for vertex- or cell-centered data [S1].
+*   **Limitations:**
+    *   Boundary quality is lower due to lack of scalar interpolation [S1].
+    *   Approximation error exists, mitigated by footprint reshaping and compositing [S1].
+    *   Fragment generation can be high if tetrahedra sizes vary widely, requiring expensive culling [S1].
+*   **When to use:** Interactive visualization of large, unstructured tetrahedral datasets, especially when connectivity processing is a bottleneck or when dealing with malformed meshes [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** When high-fidelity boundary rendering is critical and depth complexity is low, or when tetrahedra sizes vary extremely widely and culling overhead becomes prohibitive [S1].
 
 ## Background
-PBVR was introduced in a paper by the authors of [S1] to address the challenge of interactively visualizing large, unstructured tetrahedral meshes. It builds on the principles of point-based rendering and projected tetrahedra, aiming to combine the speed of point splatting with the accuracy of volume rendering. The method was applied to datasets such as Heart, SPX, fighter, and F16 [S1].
+*   **Origin:** The method was introduced in the paper "Hardware-Assisted Point-Based Volume Rendering of Tetrahedral Meshes" by the authors of [S1].
+*   **History:** It was developed to address the difficulties of interactive visualization of large, unstructured tetrahedral meshes, building on previous work in point-based rendering and splatting. It was funded by the Department of Energy under the ASCI VIEWS program, the MICS office, the National Science Foundation, Sandia National Laboratories, Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory, an IBM Faculty Award, and a University of Utah Seed Grant [S1].
+*   **Usage:** Applied to large unstructured tetrahedral datasets such as Heart, SPX, fighter, and F16 for interactive visualization [S1].
 
